1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,380
Hey, this is James Kettle from Portswigger and I just wanted to show off a cool new feature

2
00:00:11,380 --> 00:00:14,040
that I've just added to Turbo Intruder.

3
00:00:14,040 --> 00:00:22,200
So, in this window here you can see I've run a folder brute force attack on Portswigger.net

4
00:00:22,200 --> 00:00:24,660
and we've sent a few thousand requests.

5
00:00:24,660 --> 00:00:30,960
So, the challenge now is to find the interesting results like what files has it found, what folders,

6
00:00:30,960 --> 00:00:35,720
what other kind of weird behaviour and maybe like front-end server mappings have we got.

7
00:00:35,720 --> 00:00:42,060
Maybe there's different back-end servers, maybe there aren't, maybe there's some interesting cash rules only on certain paths.

8
00:00:42,060 --> 00:00:45,660
This is all stuff that I'd love to find in this table.

9
00:00:45,660 --> 00:00:51,500
But doing this manually can be quite painful because there's a whole lot of responses here.

10
00:00:51,500 --> 00:00:58,260
There's three thousand, right? And the classic approach to doing this is to sort by one column,

11
00:00:58,260 --> 00:01:03,840
like maybe the length and then kind of scroll through and then sort by a different column and so on.

12
00:01:03,840 --> 00:01:07,460
But this approach is labour intensive and takes ages.

13
00:01:07,460 --> 00:01:11,400
So, I've just added a super cool new feature called Anomaly Rank.

14
00:01:11,400 --> 00:01:26,500
What this does is it uses a local AI-free algorithm to calculate and rank every single response for how unique that response is, how anomalous it is.

15
00:01:26,500 --> 00:01:31,060
So, the higher the score, the more anomalous it is.

16
00:01:31,060 --> 00:01:37,820
So, if we hit that, we can instantly see now we've got all the interesting things at the top of our table.

17
00:01:37,820 --> 00:01:44,000
And the cool thing is this algorithm can spot some really subtle and interesting things.

18
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:51,500
It is just, it just has this kind of knack for finding valuable stuff, such as the fact that we can immediately see here,

19
00:01:51,500 --> 00:01:57,260
if you hit slash 404, then you get a 200 status code, which is quite weird.

20
00:01:57,260 --> 00:02:02,260
And once again, if you hit slash error, you also get a 200.

21
00:02:02,260 --> 00:02:06,840
And there's a whole bunch of really interesting things here.

22
00:02:06,840 --> 00:02:11,260
I'd encourage you to give it a go for yourself on one of your websites.

23
00:02:11,260 --> 00:02:17,320
It can spot things like all the different types of 404 pages and it just flags them.

24
00:02:17,320 --> 00:02:22,560
And basically, when you run an attack like this, the rare stuff is the interesting stuff.

25
00:02:22,560 --> 00:02:25,300
That's the stuff that you want to manually look at.

26
00:02:25,300 --> 00:02:28,220
And this just saves you a bunch of pain in finding those things.

27
00:02:29,120 --> 00:02:32,360
Also, this algorithm happens to be really good for AI.

28
00:02:32,620 --> 00:02:39,100
Because if you give an AI 2,000 or 3,000 HP responses, that's going to blow up the context window.

29
00:02:39,100 --> 00:02:41,960
And it won't manage to do anything useful with that whatsoever.

30
00:02:42,520 --> 00:02:50,460
Whereas with this, you just give it the top 20 results as dictated per the anomaly rank.

31
00:02:50,620 --> 00:02:53,780
And great, now it's got something that it can actually cope with.

32
00:02:53,860 --> 00:02:57,300
And it can just focus at looking at the interesting stuff.

33
00:02:57,800 --> 00:03:00,040
So yeah, I hope you find this useful.

34
00:03:00,640 --> 00:03:06,260
Turbo Intruder will actually now sort by this column by default when the attack finishes

35
00:03:06,260 --> 00:03:10,480
to reduce the amount of interaction you have to do, as you can see here.

36
00:03:10,960 --> 00:03:13,300
But if you don't like that, that's fair enough.

37
00:03:13,400 --> 00:03:14,120
I understand that.

38
00:03:14,240 --> 00:03:17,700
So in the code, you can use table.setorder.

39
00:03:17,700 --> 00:03:22,420
And then it will automatically sort by any column that you'd rather it used.

40
00:03:23,280 --> 00:03:24,140
I hope that's useful.

41
00:03:24,480 --> 00:03:26,100
Let me know how you find it.

42
00:03:26,400 --> 00:03:27,040
Thanks for listening.

43
